{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:dc7ce016ad9f77a6", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction Rate Securities", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "ARS are debt or preferred equity securities that have interest rates that are periodically re-set through auctions, typically every 7, 14, 28, or 35 days.", "question": null, "answer": "Auction rate securities (ARS) are debt or preferred equity investments whose interest rates are reset through periodic auctions, often every 7, 14, 28, or 35 days. Although they were marketed to some retail investors as cash-equivalent investments, they did not offer the same level of liquidity as money market mutual funds or certificates of deposit. If an auction attracts too few bidders, it may fail, and investors may be unable to sell their ARS until an auction succeeds.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1151, "output_tokens": 301}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:dc7ce016ad9f77a6", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction Rate Securities", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "interest-rate auctions for ARS began to fail when the auctions attracted too few bidders to establish a clearing rate.", "question": "How can too few bidders at an auction affect an Auction Rate Security?", "answer": "When too few bidders participate to establish a clearing rate, the auction can fail. Investors may then face a high or penalty interest rate, and they may be unable to sell their ARS until an auction succeeds.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1151, "output_tokens": 160}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:dc7ce016ad9f77a6", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction Rate Securities", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "although ARS did not have the same level of liquidity as those other instruments.", "question": "Misconception: Because auction rate securities were marketed as “cash-equivalent,” they were just as liquid as money market mutual funds or certificates of deposit. Is that correct?", "answer": "No. ARS were marketed to investors seeking a cash-equivalent investment, but they did not have the same level of liquidity as money market mutual funds or certificates of deposit. When auctions failed, investors could be unable to sell their ARS until a successful auction occurred.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1151, "output_tokens": 241}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3b2fa85f552ce990", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction-Based Interest Rate Resets", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "ARS are debt or preferred equity securities that have interest rates that are periodically re-set through auctions, typically every 7, 14, 28, or 35 days.", "question": null, "answer": "Auction Rate Securities (ARS) reset their interest rates through periodic auctions, often every 7, 14, 28, or 35 days. The auction is intended to establish a clearing rate for the next interest-rate period. If too few bidders participate to establish that rate, an auction can fail; the result may be a high or penalty interest rate and/or difficulty selling the securities until an auction succeeds.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1154, "output_tokens": 242}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3b2fa85f552ce990", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction-Based Interest Rate Resets", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "interest rates that are periodically re-set through auctions, typically every 7, 14, 28, or 35 days.", "question": "How often are auction rate securities’ interest rates typically reset through auctions?", "answer": "They are typically reset every 7, 14, 28, or 35 days.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1154, "output_tokens": 150}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:3b2fa85f552ce990", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction-Based Interest Rate Resets", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "These dislocations resulted in high or “penalty” interest rates on those securities and/or an inability of investors to sell their ARS until there was a successful auction.", "question": "Misconception: Because auction rate securities have their interest rates reset regularly, investors can always sell them at the next reset.", "answer": "A rate reset does not guarantee that investors can sell. If too few bidders participate to establish a clearing rate, an auction can fail, and investors may be unable to sell their securities until an auction succeeds.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1154, "output_tokens": 239}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:19972365b3980724", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Failed Auctions and Liquidity Risk", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "These dislocations resulted in high or “penalty” interest rates on those securities and/or an inability of investors to sell their ARS until there was a successful auction.", "question": null, "answer": "An auction for an auction rate security can fail when too few bidders participate to establish a clearing rate. A failed auction may lead to a high or penalty interest rate, and investors may be unable to sell their securities until an auction succeeds. This creates liquidity risk: even though ARS rates are periodically reset, investors may not be able to access their money when they want to, while the securities can have long-term maturities.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1154, "output_tokens": 235}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:19972365b3980724", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Failed Auctions and Liquidity Risk", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "an inability of investors to sell their ARS until there was a successful auction", "question": "What liquidity risk could investors face when an auction for auction rate securities fails?", "answer": "Investors may be unable to sell their ARS until a later auction succeeds, leaving them with an illiquid investment in the meantime.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1154, "output_tokens": 163}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:19972365b3980724", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Failed Auctions and Liquidity Risk", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "These dislocations resulted in high or “penalty” interest rates on those securities and/or an inability of investors to sell their ARS until there was a successful auction.", "question": "Misconception: If an auction for an auction rate security fails, investors can still sell the security whenever they want. What is the correction?", "answer": "A failed auction can leave investors unable to sell their ARS until an auction succeeds. It may also result in a high or “penalty” interest rate, so ARS can carry liquidity risk despite their periodic rate resets.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1154, "output_tokens": 228}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d98b3b8bba6cf6f9", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "The 2008 Auction Rate Securities Market Collapse", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The ARS market collapsed in February 2008 when lead underwriters chose not to step in to support the auctions.", "question": null, "answer": "The 2008 Auction Rate Securities (ARS) market collapse happened after auctions began failing because too few bidders showed up to set an interest rate. In February 2008, lead underwriters stopped stepping in to support the auctions. As a result, investors could be unable to sell their ARS until an auction succeeded, leaving them with illiquid investments despite the securities having long-term maturities.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1157, "output_tokens": 206}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d98b3b8bba6cf6f9", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "The 2008 Auction Rate Securities Market Collapse", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "The ARS market collapsed in February 2008 when lead underwriters chose not to step in to support the auctions.", "question": "What precipitated the February 2008 Auction Rate Securities market collapse, and what was its impact on investors?", "answer": "The collapse occurred when lead underwriters stopped supporting auctions. With too few bidders, auctions could fail, leaving investors unable to sell their ARS until a successful auction and holding illiquid investments with long-term maturities.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1157, "output_tokens": 232}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d98b3b8bba6cf6f9", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "The 2008 Auction Rate Securities Market Collapse", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "The ARS market collapsed in February 2008 when lead underwriters chose not to step in to support the auctions.", "question": "Misconception: The 2008 ARS market collapse happened because the securities’ long-term maturities suddenly changed. Is that correct?", "answer": "No. The source attributes the collapse to lead underwriters not stepping in to support auctions. Auctions had begun to fail when too few bidders participated to establish a clearing rate, leaving some investors unable to sell their ARS until an auction succeeded.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1157, "output_tokens": 227}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a46a2f5d3ad0a96f", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction Rate Securities Buybacks and Redemptions", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "These settlements included agreements to buy back ARS from certain investors. In addition, many issuers of ARS have redeemed their ARS and converted to other types of securities.", "question": null, "answer": "Auction rate securities (ARS) buybacks and redemptions were ways some ARS were taken back after the market collapsed. Settlements with major broker-dealers and other entities included agreements to buy back ARS from certain investors. Separately, many issuers redeemed their ARS and converted to other types of securities. These actions did not apply to every ARS or investor, so they should not be understood as a guarantee that all holders could sell or redeem their securities.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1157, "output_tokens": 323}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a46a2f5d3ad0a96f", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction Rate Securities Buybacks and Redemptions", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "These settlements included agreements to buy back ARS from certain investors.", "question": "What actions were taken to address some investors’ auction rate securities after the market collapse?", "answer": "Settlements with broker-dealers and other entities included agreements to buy back ARS from certain investors. Separately, many ARS issuers redeemed their securities and converted to other types of securities.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1157, "output_tokens": 203}}
{"id": "6183efa83a15edd0:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:a46a2f5d3ad0a96f", "source_id": "6183efa83a15edd0", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/auction-rate-securities", "topic": "Auction Rate Securities", "subtopic": "Auction Rate Securities Buybacks and Redemptions", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "These settlements included agreements to buy back ARS from certain investors.  In addition, many issuers of ARS have redeemed their ARS and converted to other types of securities.", "question": "Misconception: After the ARS market collapsed, every investor was automatically bought out or had their securities redeemed.", "answer": "The source does not say that every investor was bought out or redeemed. It says that settlements included buyback agreements for certain investors, and that many issuers redeemed their ARS and converted them to other types of securities.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1157, "output_tokens": 274}}
